{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested on exact I1 source head 97518f68eaae88591a1aead854d69023577c1268, direct parent d845b59b8db6e203dda4a56bad88eae7991cd645 and base main b2a0bb12dc3900b1cc71e5884683f0e121c6af66. Ordinary complete 156-subject review under 2186c3c2/aa78f7f5, invitation 2e30335d and checker promise 63ed94b7. Three P2 findings remain; two are already owned by 5fc07d88. No duplicate repair commission is needed.\n\nP2 — Preserve supported names in prototype-safe dictionaries. The validator accepts nonempty own JSON names, including __proto__ (derive/src/validate.ts:156–161 and 666–674), but frame.ts:43–50 normalizes fields into {} with fields[name] = value. A required text field named __proto__ with a valid string passes the field check and loses its own value through the inherited prototype setter. effects.ts:33–35 trusts that declared field's type and reads Object.prototype; its value effect and fold.ts:77 then put an object in the text slot. bytes/src/canonical.ts:71–81 can seal that value as {}. The signed value has been changed into a value outside the declared slot type, contrary to adopted R1's slot invariant.\n\nA separate consequence of the same dictionary convention is validate.ts:777–778: assigning an item type named __proto__ into deadlines = {} changes its prototype instead of creating an enumerable deadline row. reserve.ts:43–44 omits that type. With an untimed genesis at sequence 0, one separately opened item at sequence 1, and two finite timed rules A -\u003e B -\u003e final, scopeEntries = 3 admits the opening: it incorrectly counts two used entries plus only one closing checkpoint. Correct admission needs five entries: genesis, opening, both timed transitions and the checkpoint. After the deadline passes, turn.ts:212 admits the first transition at sequence 2 and permanently blocks the second at sequence 3. The original declaration still supplies the due rules; the reservation omission does not disable selection. Two independent reviewers and root confirmed these source paths statically. Use safe own-property construction or maps consistently for externally chosen names, and own-property grammar lookups. Preserve supported names rather than introduce an unexplained name restriction. Reuse compact parsed-JSON fixtures for the text-copy and finite-chain admission boundaries. No evaluator or runtime probe was run. The related inherited FIELD_SHAPES lookup can throw, but core.ts:92–98 contains that failure; this finding does not claim an unhandled founding failure.\n\nP2 — Finish contract-defined nested reply validation under existing 5fc07d88. client/src/answers.ts:47 accepts {effect:\"state\"} without item or state and accepts unknown effect names, although contract/src/entry.ts defines a closed union with required fields. Lines 50–52 accept an act input without signed intent or authority, an unknown input type, uses:[{}], prepared:[{}] and sends:[{}] inside otherwise valid entries. Lines 54–56 accept attributed:[{}] and malformed party members. Those are fixed Input, FactUse, Prepared, Send and MemberRef/Party records, not opaque application payloads. Lines 22–25 and 76 also allow source-unverified and unsupported-definition on submitted-act refusals, although Answer excludes both and Founded permits them. Both HTTP and binding use this checker and return the result as a typed outcome. Outer-field repairs and compile-time tables are useful, but neither a source comment nor delta 152 makes these fixed nested records opaque. Validate their recognized variants and required surrounding members, and distinguish the operation's refusal union. Leave genuinely unknown request bodies, evidence and application values opaque; add no second judge or authenticity claim. Preserve exact-intent retry and receipt recovery. Correct the primary and builder assertion's complete reply-validation claim when reporting the actual remaining boundary.\n\nP2 — Bound client HTTP ingestion and waiting under the same existing 5fc07d88 scope. client/src/http.ts:17 offers text() without a byte bound, deadline or cancellation; lines 34–36 await fetch and its entire body before parsing at line 42 or validating at line 46. An oversized body is fully allocated first; a fetch or body that never completes leaves the call pending without a transport failure. The complete adapter correction explicitly owes consumption and wait/cancellation bounds at this boundary. Enforce stated limits while reading, before whole-body allocation and parsing, and give stalled/over-limit reads an honest transport failure that preserves the uncertainty of a submitted intent. The source's RPC answer disposal is present; no new concrete RPC lifetime defect is claimed here. Whole-call and stalled-binding policy remain with existing owners. The replay transport has separate bounded streaming, page/retained limits and deadlines; its repair does not bound this client adapter.\n\nArchitecture: The six active packages form a coherent shared contract, byte custody, pure derivation, transactional scope runtime, independent verifier and typed client. The explicit-stack timer graph repair traverses distinct nodes and edges, preserving cycle refusal and finite chain counts; it structurally resolves e1ce2d76's path-multiplicity defect. Earlier receipt identity, detached signing snapshot, replay entry-byte limit, Node engine range, party-member sizing, raw Worker input limit, malformed URI containment and closing-checkpoint/rearming corrections are structurally present. Source readings establish those candidate repairs, not independent execution or a separate correction delivery. Declarations and complete capacity dimensions, creator closure/liveness, expanded forms, authority, browser/workspace/device continuity, provider settlement and full component retirement remain owned. No deferred sibling interface or source choice is adopted by landing this implementation.\n\nSecurity: Production authority and reader defaults deny access. Delivery checks bind fetched facts to the actual source send; commits retain admitted entries and their inputs atomically. The supported-name corruption and malformed typed replies must be repaired. Neither the source nor this review proves production authorization, provider effects or resistance to injected member text. Unknown effects and cleanup must survive later application integration. Integrity checks its chain/hash/signature scope with judgments explicitly trusted; the shallow integrity input guard is not a demonstrated failure of those guarantees. Narrowing its README's “shape of an entry” wording to the inspected envelope is a nonblocking precision correction.\n\nSimplification: Keep one canonical implementation and the shared judges/fold/answer boundary. A consistent safe dictionary convention removes both name-handling faults without another mechanism. Repair the existing adapters with compact invariant witnesses; no heavy extra gate, legacy adapter, prior-format reader or obsolete test restoration is requested.\n\nCoverage: All 156 submitted current subjects, totaling 1,772,645 bytes, were freshly read completely through EOF by delegated reviewers, with all truncated ranges recovered. Partition: bytes/derive 51 files and 390,682 bytes; scope 35 and 225,921; client/contract 27 and 91,744; docs/notes 8 and 499,550; replay/root/tooling/submitted parked material 35 and 564,748. The last group includes four inactive guides fully read by two additional delegated reviewers. Every changed byte for submitted subjects was covered, including removed client/contract bodies and all removed lock records. Root independently inspected every complete artifact statement and effective decision, the full invitation and originating commission, and the ordinary assigned binding with delivery 90a6995e first. Root freshly read full frame/effects/fold/reserve/canonical/verifier/client answers/HTTP/binding/entry/result and delivery subjects, plus targeted validation/timer drain and adopted R1 invariants; root does not claim a personal 156-file read.\n\nRetirement inspection distinguishes 371 byte-identical R100 moves, eight submitted inactive guide changes and native rename-aware deletion counts from the logical 220 replaced-path ledger. Omitted pure move/deletion bodies were metadata-only, except deleted client/contract bodies separately read by their reviewer. The five-fix carried blobs are preserved. Parking does not settle deployed spike, packed release, CI/installer/provider retargeting or later I2/I3/I5 duties. Six historical notes/cloud-reviews/scripts diagnostic bodies were neither read nor executed and are not among the submitted 156 subjects.\n\nUseful-test economy remains the highest implementation priority. The producer reports a 203-test/typecheck gate at this head; the delivery's cost table is explicitly an earlier c2ea17ce single-run observation against a different whole-product scope. Neither is independent checker execution or comparable whole-product tenfold proof. The 152 unique I1 choices retain adopted R1 dispositions through 114 and existing successor 108ea663 ownership thereafter. Correct stale count/status prose without reducing those duties. Static source, document, hash and provenance review only: no tests, builds, installs, project or private diagnostics, provider effects, source edits, deployment, merge or publication. Return a corrected ordinary complete source candidate under the existing I1 request; this verdict approves no landing or implementation closure.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:90a6995eafec6e3cba3166e849d50771a9f6a689","binding":"assigned","head":"97518f68eaae88591a1aead854d69023577c1268","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:63ed94b7ba5be2ce09447d822080c740bf3f43a3\",\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:5ea2770e43c05a8d8b6760aa1a62a87aefce12de\",\"git:sha1:589bca982736a23